{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA progress 2026-09-09: Phase 1 observation reconstructed from durable CI/Gerrit evidence. Failed run 34235734839 was change 2834 patchset 1. The failing job was require resolvable rebar ticket, and its checkout log shows the external checkout-gerrit-change-action first checked out the workflow dispatch ref from GitHub (`ref: 665d1d0480187380f82e59bd95af1f0c44274732`, the main/workflow ref), then tried `git fetch origin refs/changes/34/2834/1` (GitHub mirror does not advertise Gerrit refs), then fell back to `git fetch https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/rebar refs/changes/34/2834/1`, which failed immediately with HTTP 502. Gerrit later recovered; recheck run 34236969595 passed Verified, and change 2834 ultimately merged as 02e0fae923d45329a3e1dc1921d70e19484401b8. Working comparison: the patchset commit SHA itself (`d09bfdc154fe825ab0f8865c78f4f2c4f70c4590`) is fetchable from the GitHub mirror by object id, while `refs/changes/...` is not. Hypothesis H1: the failure class is not an invalid commit or bad trailer; it is the CI checkout helper treating Gerrit ref fetch as mandatory even when the patchset object is already mirrored by SHA, so a transient Gerrit HTTP 502/504 can fail Verified before any repository validation. Next experiment/fix should make Verified checkout prefer `GERRIT_PATCHSET_REVISION` from the GitHub mirror and only use Gerrit refspec as fallback, while verifying any fallback FETCH_HEAD equals the dispatch SHA."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788921200506892001,"uuid":"fd0267b4-a7f5-4d03-a322-ee18e7f40b46"}